m  affects everyone involved and that is  including the families left behind  um to show how strong  a family can be to to support each other  the friendships the female friendships  um you know we see so many stories of  the prisoners  but you know this the it also affects  everybody outside that  is involved and also continues on also  with you know  um benjamin um earl turner's character  um he plays earl and you know he's on  probation and it  and it's still he's out of prison but it  still haunts him  after he's after he's out and it's hard  for him  to to live in this world  out you know outside of prison so um i  just think  it's it's an it's a it's a perspective  that i think um is  interesting for people to find out more  about  and to get more of an understanding that  it's not just  inside these prison walls it affects all  the loved ones that  that are involved and trickles down  um and you know how they deal with that  trauma how they deal with that  depression  all of that is is incredibly powerful  and i think that comes through  in the show um but one of the things  about the show that i love is that it's  unlike anything else like it's  about serious issues and it's funny  but then i'm like not sure if i'm  supposed to be laughing sometimes  and i'm like this is ridiculous and  scary but and disturbing but also  hilarious and beautiful  um can you talk about just the tone of  the show because i think it's so  unique and original there's spoken word  where you're talking  you're breaking that fourth wall there's  choreography um  and it's so oakland and that comes  across in every single frame so just  talk about how all of that comes  together and why  it's it's a unique portrayal of that  family experience on the outside of a  prison that you were talking about  i'm so proud of the show and the way  we handle the comedy and also you know  the important issues that we talk about  in the show it is entertaining and  you know you are supposed to laugh and  but i think that's also kind of  the reality of life you know people  people laugh through their trauma people  deal with traumas in so many different  ways  and you know i my it follows my  character ashley and she goes into  these heightened verse sequences and  you know i had to kind of analyze them  almost like shakespearean monologues  where she really opens up  you know with the audience it's her most  honest and truest self and then  you know you have the choreography in  the show which is almost like  a greek chorus in a play and  you know they're basically dancing our  feelings and  showing an another way another creative  way to express  what is happening in the scene um  and kind of you know this this haunting  idea of the prison system and that it  never leaves you  once you are involved with it in some  sort of way shape or form and i think  you know we are  us as actors we are like these emotional  vessels to really show the humanity  of of what happens and and you know all  of these feelings that we have that are  complicated and complex and especially  through  you know the female point of view um  um to show that my my character ashley  she is  she's an amazing mother she's a strong  mother she'll do anything for her family  but she'll also break down you know  or track a hotel room or you know um  break the fourth knowledge where how  she's feeling with the audience and  that is so human and such a beautiful  way to  to show the complexities of of what  happens to these people and you know in  these situations  it's so funny that you say that you have  to sort of find the comedy  in the trauma because as i sit here in  the middle of this pandemic  i have often asked myself the question  like i have never lived through a  pandemic before  but like when i i see a funny meme about  the pandemic or  or even like the insurrection i'm like i  wonder if people  in history made funny memes and jokes  about sort of the trauma they were  experiencing  how have you been processing what  what you were all going through i mean  you're making incredible art  during one of i think for everyone no  matter what you do  the weirdest time in history  um honestly every day is different  you know i really can't sit here and and  be like i do this  all the time because i don't it changes  you know i'm  i'm grateful and and so thankful that  i've been able to work in this pandemic  and  and give people some sort of  entertainment and another world to  escape to  i like to watch you know a lot of  movies and tvs to to escape  and and try to have you know spend time  if i can with my loved ones and my  friends as much as possible  you know it's really this pandemic has  shown me the you know the value and how  important relationships  are you know especially at the beginning  when  when we were taken or that was taken  away from us you know i didn't know when  i was gonna  be able to see my mom or my dad you know  next i  i had no idea so you know every day is  different i think we all handle it  in many different ways some more  responsible than others  um but it's it's a day-by-day process  you and your dad made history by being  the first  father-daughter duo to win emmys in the  same year and you're halfway to egot  already and you're only 32. so  what what is your like goal sheet look  like  at this point what are you striving for  oh man i you know i i never want to put  myself in a box there's  there's so much that i want to do i i  just went away for a week  a week and wrote a whole album  um i want to focus on my music at the  moment  um i'm i'd love to  continue to produce and and create my  own material i have a bunch of ideas and  and stuff that i've already been working  on  for other tv shows and movies that i  want to create  so i think it's kind of endless and you  know whatever  keeps pushing me as an artist and keeps  challenging me  you know that's that's what i want to do  i never want to fully feel comfortable  i love that it's like being an artist  there's just limitless potential you  just  you keep going and you keep pushing um  and you keep  creating it's beautiful jasmine c fish  jones thank you so much for taking the  time  and congratulations on the show it's  really really um  original and and fascinating and i hope  people check it out  thank you so much thank you for having  me

